

⭐ VIEWPORT — Full Specification

The Viewport is built from four functional layers, stacked like a simulation sandwich:

Base Media Layer — the cinematic video or frame sequence

Overlay Canvas — all visual intelligence (Θ, Ξ, organisms, metric)

HUD Layer — text, stats, blueprint snippets

Interaction Layer — zoom, pan, selection, probes

Each layer is independent, composable, and GPU‑accelerated.

🔶 1. Base Media Layer

This is the raw cinematic output from your engine.

Responsibilities:

Render video frames at native resolution

Maintain aspect ratio

Support high‑FPS playback (60–240 fps)

Provide pixel‑accurate coordinates to overlays

Features:

Hardware decoding

Frame stepping

High‑precision seeking

Resolution scaling (fit, fill, 1:1, 2:1)

This layer is the visual foundation.

🔶 2. Overlay Canvas

This is where the simulation intelligence is drawn.

It is a transparent canvas rendered on top of the media layer.

Supports:

Heatmaps

Vector fields

Contours

Region masks

Organism outlines

Metric grids

Lightcones

Regime maps

Rendering pipeline:

Receive current time from SyncController

Pull data from DataChannel

Render each active overlay layer in order

Composite using blending modes

Performance:

GPU‑accelerated

Batched draw calls

Dynamic resolution scaling

This layer is the scientific brain of the viewport.

🔶 3. HUD Layer

This is the informational overlay — text, stats, and blueprint metadata.

HUD elements:

Current time t

Frame index

Δτ (experienced time)

Active time model

Local Θ value

Local Ξ regime

Organism count

Meta‑equation state

Blueprint snippet (optional)

HUD modes:

Minimal — time + frame

Scientific — full telemetry

Blueprint — DSL code + regime markers

This layer is the narrator of the simulation.

🔶 4. Interaction Layer

This is where the user touches the simulation.

Supported interactions:

Zoom

Scroll wheel

Pinch gesture

Zoom to cursor

Reset zoom

Pan

Click + drag

Middle‑mouse drag

Arrow keys

Selection

Click to probe a point

Drag to select a region

Shift‑click to add multiple probes

Probe Readout

When you click a point, you get:

Θ value

Ξ regime

Metric curvature

Organism influence

Δτ at that point

Blueprint line responsible

This layer makes the viewport interactive and alive.

⭐ Viewport Modes

The viewport supports three major modes, each optimized for a different purpose.

🔷 1. Cinematic Mode

For pure visual playback.

No overlays

Minimal HUD

Smoothest performance

Used for showcase videos.

🔷 2. Scientific Mode

For deep analysis.

All overlays available

Full HUD

Probe tools enabled

Regime boundaries visible

Used for debugging the time fabric.

🔷 3. Blueprint Mode

For understanding the code → output relationship.

Blueprint snippet pinned

Regime markers highlighted

Meta‑equation triggers pulsing

Organism events labeled

Used for designing and validating blueprints.

⭐ Viewport API (Engine‑Facing)

The viewport exposes a clean API to the rest of the viewer:

Viewport.loadMedia(source)
Viewport.setOverlays(list<OverlayLayer>)
Viewport.setHUDMode(mode)
Viewport.setZoom(level)
Viewport.setPan(x, y)
Viewport.render(time, dataState)
Viewport.probe(x, y) -> ProbeResult

This makes it modular, testable, and engine‑agnostic.



